WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.800
Three engravings, each drawn by a program from a few rules.

2
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:11.000
A fern. Four rules, each a small affine map: shrink, turn, shift.

3
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:17.500
Start anywhere. Pick a rule at random, apply it to the last point, mark the result. Repeat.

4
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:24.000
The dice are loaded: 85 in 100 for the frond, 7 each for the two leaflets, 1 for the stem.

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.500
No rule mentions a leaflet. Each leaflet is a smaller copy of the whole fern.

6
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:36.800
334,176 points. The same four rules every time.

7
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:43.000
A sunflower. One rule: turn, place a seed, step out a little.

8
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Seed n sits at an angle of n × 137.508°, at a distance from the centre that grows as √n.

9
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:58.000
The turn is the golden angle, the turn least like any simple fraction of a circle. So no seed lines up with an earlier one.

10
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:06.000
619 seeds. No rule draws the spirals. They appear anyway.

11
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Turn 135° instead, three eighths of a circle, and the seeds fall into eight straight rows.

12
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Turn 137°, half a degree short, and they curl into arms with gaps between them.

13
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.800
Back to 137.508°. No gaps.

14
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:22.000
The moon. A ball, a rough map of seas and craters, and one rule for light.

15
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.000
The sun lights one half. At midnight UTC on 23 October 2026, the moon stood about 139° from the sun, seen from Earth.

16
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:34.000
So 88% of the face we see is lit: waxing gibbous. The dark side is drawn faint, so the face stays legible.

17
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.800
The seas are placed by hand and the craters at random. The light follows the rule.

18
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:45.800
Rules first. The form follows.
